<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_(OpenStax)/25:_Biomolecules_-_Carbohydrates/25.15:_Additional_Problems" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="Mechanism_Problems"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-459682 editable"><strong><span class="red-text">Mechanism Problems</span></strong></h3>

<p><strong>PROBLEM 25.30&nbsp;</strong>Galactose, one of the eight essential monosaccharides (<b>Section 25.7</b>), is biosynthesized from UDP-glucose by galactose 4-epimerase, where UDP = uridylyl diphosphate (a ribonucleotide diphosphate; <b>Section 28.1</b>). The enzyme requires NAD<sup>+</sup> for activity (<b>Section 17.7</b>), but it is not a stoichiometric reactant, and NADH is not a final reaction product. Propose a mechanism.</p>

<p><img alt="u=" data-height="262" data-width="1145" style="width: 954px; height: 218px;" width="954px" height="218px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/462391/3d263276b337b69fa932d4f26bebe06a749c1130?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=954&amp;height=218" /></p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM 25.31&nbsp;</strong>Mannose, one of the eight essential monosaccharides (<b>Section 25.7</b>), is biosynthesized as its 6-phosphate derivative from fructose 6-phosphate. No enzyme cofactor is required. Propose a mechanism.</p>

<p><img alt="mannose,=" data-height="242" data-width="692" style="width: 612px; height: 214px;" width="612px" height="214px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/462392/de8f62d9dfc940bd61787eb275a8231d83f0055c?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=612&amp;height=214" /></p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM 25.32&nbsp;</strong>Glucosamine, one of the eight essential monosaccharides (<b>Section 25.7</b>), is biosynthesized as its 6-phosphate derivative from fructose 6-phosphate by reaction with ammonia. Propose a mechanism.</p>

<p><img alt="glucosamine,=" data-height="242" data-width="728" style="width: 586px; height: 195px;" width="586px" height="195px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/462398/6f312693fe5776357b9f3e22994a75d13943787c?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=586&amp;height=195" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM 25.33&nbsp;</strong>D-Glucose reacts with acetone in the presence of acid to yield the nonreducing 1,2&thinsp;:&thinsp;5,6-diisopropylidene-D-glucofuranose. Propose a mechanism.</p>

<p><img alt="The reaction of the Haworth projection of D-glucose with acetone in the presence of hydrochloric acid to yield the non-reducing 1, 2 : 5, 6 diisopropylidene D-glucofuranose." data-height="379" data-width="686" style="width: 567px; height: 313px;" width="567px" height="313px" class="scaled-down internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/462401/f1bbf0c285c0334fc84d5c0afbad6e72c8d44b6c?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=567&amp;height=313" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM 25.34&nbsp;</strong>One of the steps in the biological pathway for carbohydrate metabolism is the conversion of fructose 1,6-bisphosphate into dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate. Propose a mechanism for the transformation.</p>

<p><img alt="the=" data-height="340" data-width="661" style="width: 548px; height: 282px;" width="548px" height="282px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/462400/8f1e0606da11e6f5e1f6f1683efc25d39e1cc689?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=548&amp;height=282" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM 25.35&nbsp;</strong><span class="&lt;p">L-Fucose, one of the eight essential monosaccharides (<b>Section 25.7</b>), is biosynthesized from GDP-D-mannose by the following three-step reaction sequence, where GDP = guanosine diphosphate (a ribonucleoside diphosphate; <b>Section 28.1</b>): </span></p>

<p><span class="&lt;p"><img alt="G D P- L-Fucose, one of the essential monosaccharides, is biosynthesized from G D P - D-mannose by a three-step reaction sequence." data-height="488" data-width="1152" style="width: 968px; height: 411px;" width="968px" height="411px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/462407/6ad1794b66642e96fbf482fd5b84897cc424fe4f?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=968&amp;height=411" /> </span></p>

<p><span class="&lt;p">(a) </span>Step 1 involves an oxidation to a ketone, a dehydration to an enone, and a conjugate reduction. The step requires NADP<sup>+</sup>, but no NADPH is formed as a final reaction product. Propose a mechanism.</p>

<p>(b) Step 2 accomplishes two epimerizations and utilizes acidic and basic sites in the enzyme but does not require a coenzyme. Propose a mechanism.</p>

<p>(c) Step 3 requires NADPH as a coenzyme. Show the mechanism.</p>

